Jeremy Corbyn the world is going through  a terrifying public health crisis it's  now very quickly turning into a huge  economic crisis as well if things have  been different in that general election  you'd have been the man steering us  through how would you have handled it  hope we'd have started onto it more  quickly and recognized the seriousness  of it from the World Health Organization  warnings that were sent out in January  from China and recognized that the only  way you can get on top of it is by  knowing how severe it is and that means  testing the fact that we are not yet  even testing 10,000 people a day is very  very serious indeed  there are almost half a million people  working in the NHS and the care sector  even they have not yet been tested it is  ludicrous we've got to get on top of  testing so just to be clear you don't  think it's about the speed and scale of  the crisis and the growth of the  epidemic you think rather the testing  failures have been because the  government's dropped the ball it's both  it has obviously been a very fast-moving  crisis we do have to bring it under  control  testing is a very very important way of  it but also the superhuman efforts of  people throughout the NHS to free up  beds to bring staff back to bring  retired staff back and now to open three  new hospitals is wonderful and we should  applaud them but we shouldn't have had  ten years of austerity when we ended up  with 94% bed occupancy in the NHS and  obviously a difficulty in coping because  of that when Boris Johnson says as he  did in his video address that he did  last night that the ambition is to  really ramp up test and that's what  they're really doing does it irritate  you given that that's not the reality on  the ground it irritates me a great deal  when nearly three weeks ago John  Ashworth and I met him to raise these  very points with him saying the  importance of getting on top of testing  and it seemed to me that the facilities  the government to get that testing done  have simply not been effective what did  he say he would do three weeks ago he's  that at that time they were testing at  5,000 a day and it was going to increase  as quickly as possible here we are now  all these weeks later we're not even yet  reaching 10,000 a day and it's  apparently because of the shortage of  test equipment because the NHS was not  able to purchase it when he needed to  because they seemed to be running on the  idea that it wasn't necessary to test it  it is absolutely how can you possibly  have an idea of how many people have cv  unless you test well Jeremy Corbyn there  in conversation with Beth Beth Rigby who  joins us now Beth he's critical of the  government quite strongly critical of  the government the government will say  well hang on a minute  we were just acting on scientific advice  if you criticize ours you indirectly  criticized civil servants yes well I  think the point that Jeremy Corbyn was  making in that interview on testing was  that he thought it was a ludicrous  situation that the government hadn't  ramped up testing more quickly he talked  about the examples from South Korea  talked about Germany and how they had  scaled up tested and said it was a  ludicrous situation that we find  ourselves in that those frontline NHS  staff are not getting the sort of  testing that they need and we know the  issue around this many thousands of NHS  staff that should be working on the  frontline as this disease advances and  the number of cases in hospital  continues to grow they should be at work  but they're off self isolating at home  not necessarily because they're ill but  because someone in their family is and  they can't be tested now the government  has come under huge criticism for this  in the past 24 hours and I expect that  they will be kind trying to bring  forward some sort of action plan to give  some clearer answers as to how they  intend to improve that but what was  interested in that interview was Jeremy  Corbyn was very clear that three weeks  ago he had a meeting with the Prime  Minister with the shadow health  secretary John Ashworth in which the  Prime Minister talked about trying to  ramp up test and he talked about getting  to 5,000 tests a day and scaling that up  that was three weeks ago we're still in  a situation  we're we're woefully behind other  countries and we're woefully under solve  in the NHS frontline staff the other  issue in that interview of course was  the issue of austerity and Jeremy Corbyn  is stepping down as labor leader on  Saturday we sat down together we'll run  out a full interview later on it's his  final sit-down interview that he will  give as Labour leader and he also talked  about austerity about how the NHS in his  view was underprepared going into this  crisis because it had been under strain  already before this added pressure came  to it at the other point he made in the  interview was that he didn't believe  free market economics could now solve  this argument that the coronavirus  crisis has radically changed the  relationship between all of us and the  state and the relationship between the  government and the state and that those  sorts of big investment big spending  programs that labor had put forward in  the general election as ideologically  the right thing to do is now being done  out of necessity by a Conservative  government and I talked to him about  what that meant for the Labour Party and  we'll run that out later as to what he  had to say
 

